schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Writing Grant Proposals"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A practical guide for local node builders writing Web3 grant proposals.
    Distinguishes prospective grants (fund a promise of future work) from
    retroactive grants (reward already-delivered impact, e.g. Optimism's Retro
    Funding), explains quadratic funding (QF) math and its implication that
    "your job isn't only to impress a panel, it's to turn out a lot of real
    people," lists what a working proposal contains per Gitcoin's own guidance
    (lead with a TL;DR including how the money will be spent, metrics over
    promises, show your team, iterate in public), argues that for a local node
    in a QF round the proposal IS the fundraising campaign (anchored on ReFi
    DAO's GG18 Local Node round case study), and closes with an explicit
    warning against single-source grant dependency as a burnout/turnover
    trap, recommending diversified funding.
  audience: local node builders writing their first Web3 grant proposal
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Grant as finish line vs. grant as one input — the article's central
      argument is that single-source grant dependency is a burnout/turnover
      risk, not that grants are bad.
    - >-
      Impressing a panel vs. turning out real people — in QF specifically, the
      article reframes proposal-writing as a community-organizing task, not a
      persuasive-writing task.
  related_concepts:
    - quadratic-funding
    - retroactive-public-goods-funding
  related_resources:
    - gitcoin-grants
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  high_risk: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/writing-grant-proposals.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
# - Flagged high_risk + public-use-boundary below since the article gives concrete
#   funding/governance strategy recommendations (how to structure a sustainability plan, how
#   much of a budget to make grant-dependent) per the ingest skill's funding/governance-recs
#   trigger.
# - Did not create separate resource cards for OP Atlas / Retro Funding Missions or the named
#   ReFi Medellín/ReFi Costa Rica follow-on projects — captured as part of the GG18
#   claim-evidence's narrative context rather than as independent resource subjects.
